On Tue, 2006-04-11 at 10:28 -0400, Stephen Smalley wrote:
> - Do we truly want multiple structures and parsers for file contexts in
> libsemanage (already have in src/fcontexts_record.c and
> fcontexts_file.c)? 
> - If this uses the same algorithm as fc_sort in refpolicy build, then
> why does it yield a differently ordered file_contexts when running
> semodule -b base.pp on the same base.pp file before and after applying
> the patch to libsemanage?  Not clear as to whether it actually affects
> the resulting labeling applied, but it does change the ordering of 236
> entries.

In reverse order:

There was an error in my patch that handled the escape character '\'
incorrectly.  The attached patch, modified to fix this bug, replaces the
previous patch.

It is certainly desirable to consolidate parsers and structures, but in
this case the parsers and structures available in fcontext_record.c and
fcontexts_file.c were written to work with files only, not buffers.  The
options appear to be:  

1) Use the existing parser as-is by writing an intermediate temp file of
contexts that would be immediately read back in for sorting.

2) Add separate parsers and structs, as done in the attached patch.

3) Rework the existing parsers and structs to directly support the sort
without going through the file system.

4) Find some half-way point, such as:
4a) reuse the semanage_fcontext structure in semanage_store.c, but still
use separate parsing routines added to semanage_store.c  
4b) Add new parsing routines (and possibly structs) to something other
than semanage_store.c to compliment the existing functions.

1 seems hacky. 
2 is simple and works, but possibly duplicates functionality.
3 would be pretty invasive to the existing parse_utils.c code (at
least), and seems to muddy functions that already have a clearly defined
purpose.
4a is a weak compromise.
4b is more general but I'm not clear as to where the right place is for
these functions, and to a lesser extent who else besides
semanage_store.c will use them.  Perhaps we should create an
fcontexts_buffer.c along with additions to parse_utils.c?

Thanks,
Chris

